  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What do ailerons do?",
      "a": "Ailerons are hinged panels near the wing tips that move in opposite directions. The wing with the lowered aileron gains lift and rises, the other drops, and the plane rolls. Banking like this is how a plane turns.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a plane turn?",
        "What are the flaps at the ends of the wings?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the elevator do?",
      "a": "The elevator is the hinged back edge of the horizontal tail. Tilting it up pushes the tail down and the nose up to climb; tilting it down lowers the nose.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a plane go up and down?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the rudder do?",
      "a": "The rudder is the hinged back part of the tail fin. It swings the nose left or right, called yaw, and helps keep turns balanced and the plane lined up with the runway in a crosswind.",
      "asks": [
        "What is yaw?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does the pilot move the control surfaces?",
      "a": "In older and smaller planes, cables and rods link the stick and pedals to the surfaces. In fly-by-wire airliners, the stick sends electrical signals to computers, which command hydraulic actuators to move the surfaces.",
      "asks": [
        "What is fly-by-wire?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why doesn't a plane turn with the rudder alone?",
      "a": "The rudder alone just skids the nose sideways, which is inefficient and uncomfortable. Banking with the ailerons tilts the wing's lift sideways, and that sideways part of the lift curves the plane's path.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do planes tilt when they turn?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The Airbus A320, first flown in 1987, was the first airliner with digital fly-by-wire controls.",
    "Roll, pitch and yaw are rotations about three axes through a plane's centre of gravity."
  ],
